faqs:
  - question: Why do my legs hurt when I walk but not when I rest?
    answer: >-
      Muscles need more blood when they are working. If your arteries are
      narrowed, they cannot get enough, which causes cramping pain. When you
      stop, the muscles need less blood and the pain eases. This is called
      intermittent claudication.
---

**Peripheral arterial disease (PAD)** is when a build-up of fatty deposits narrows the arteries, restricting blood flow to your **legs**. It is common, especially in people over 60 and people who smoke. People with PAD are also more likely to have a heart attack or stroke.

## Symptoms

- **pain, cramping or aching** in the calves, thighs or buttocks when walking, which goes away with rest (**intermittent claudication**)
- cold or numb feet
- hair loss on the legs and feet
- shiny, pale or bluish skin on the legs
- sores or ulcers on the feet or legs that do not heal

Many people have no symptoms at first.

## What raises the risk?

- **smoking**, which is the biggest risk
- **diabetes**
- **high blood pressure** and **high cholesterol**
- getting older

## Getting diagnosed

Your GP will check the pulses in your legs and feet, and may do an **ankle brachial pressure index (ABPI)** test, which compares the blood pressure in your ankles and arms. You may have an ultrasound scan.

Read about [treatment for peripheral arterial disease](/conditions/heart-and-circulation/peripheral-arterial-disease/treatment/).

{% callout type="danger" title="Call 999" %}
Call **999** if your leg or foot suddenly becomes **painful, cold, pale, blue or numb**, or you cannot move it. This may mean the blood supply is blocked, which is an emergency.
{% /callout %}
